With the supercharges fixed by Eq. [eq:l7-differential-supercharges], define $\mathcal D=\partial_\theta-i\bar\theta\partial_t,
\qquad
\bar{\mathcal D}=\partial_{\bar\theta}-i\theta\partial_t.$ The only change is the sign of the time-derivative term. Direct calculation gives $\begin{aligned}\{\mathcal D,q\}
&=\{\mathcal D,\bar q\}
=\{\bar{\mathcal D},q\}
=\{\bar{\mathcal D},\bar q\}=0,\\
\{\mathcal D,\bar{\mathcal D}\}&=-2i\partial_t.
\end{aligned}$ The two potentially nonzero cross terms cancel. This is the odd analogue of a covariant derivative: it differentiates while preserving the transformation law.

